# Copy to .env.local for development. Set the real values in Vercel env vars.
# Never commit .env.local. The FUB key must never reach client code. CLAUDE.md §9.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A NAME EXISTING IN VERCEL DOES NOT MEAN IT HAS A VALUE.
#
# Production returned 502 on every lead for nine days while all four variables
# below were present in `vercel env ls`. They had been created with the project,
# apparently seeded from the blank right-hand sides of this file, and an empty
# string is falsy — so every `if (!apiKey)` guard fired exactly as written.
#
# Two things make this hard to spot, and both mislead in the same direction:
#
#   - `vercel env add` answers "already exists", which reads like "already set".
#   - `vercel env ls` prints "Hidden" for a Secret whether it holds a key or "".
#
# What distinguishes empty from wrong is the error text, so read it rather than
# assuming. "is not configured" comes from the falsy guard and means empty or
# absent. A wrong key never reaches that branch — it fails at the API instead,
# as "Follow Up Boss responded 401".
#
# To repair rather than add: `vercel env rm NAME production --yes`, then
# `vercel env add NAME production`. Then redeploy — variables only apply to
# deployments created after them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Follow Up Boss.
#
# FUB_API_KEY is a personal key generated from Jasmine's own user (FUB → her
# avatar → My Settings → API). A personal key acts as that user, which is what
# we want; it also dies with the user, so if her account is ever removed from
# the brokerage this integration stops rather than silently posting as someone
# else.
FUB_API_KEY=

# Who the lead is assigned to. SET ONE OF THESE.
#
# The key lives inside the brokerage's FUB account, whose Lead Flow rules are
# configured elsewhere and can route an incoming lead — including round-robin.
# A lead this site produced should land on her, so assignment is stated rather
# than left to a default. See the header of lib/fub.ts.
#
# Prefer the numeric id: a display name changes, an id does not. Get it from
# GET https://api.followupboss.com/v1/identity, which also confirms which
# account and user the key authenticates as.
#
# Note the API host is api.followupboss.com for every account. The team logs in
# at mattstone.followupboss.com and mints keys at /2/api on that host, but that
# is the web app — see the comment on FUB_ENDPOINT in lib/fub.ts.
FUB_ASSIGNED_USER_ID=
FUB_ASSIGNED_TO=

# Registered-system identification. Optional, both or neither.
#
# The API key says who the request is for; X-System says what made it. Register
# at https://apps.followupboss.com/system-registration. Raises the rate limit
# from 125 to 250 requests per 10s (irrelevant here) and makes the integration
# identifiable in the account as this website rather than an anonymous caller —
# which is the actual reason to bother.
FUB_SYSTEM=
FUB_SYSTEM_KEY=

# Resend — transactional email fallback so a lead is never lost.
#
# Not a nice-to-have. Given who owns the CRM, this is the one record of a lead
# that does not live in the brokerage's Follow Up Boss, and app/api/lead/route.ts
# returns 502 when both channels are unconfigured — which is what production was
# doing until 2026-08-28.
#
# Easiest provisioning is the Vercel Marketplace integration, which sets
# RESEND_API_KEY itself in every environment:
#   vercel integration add resend/resend-email
#
# FROM ADDRESS. A new Resend account may only send from onboarding@resend.dev,
# and only TO the address the account was created with, until a domain is
# verified. That restriction happens to fit this exact use case — the lead
# notification goes to her — so the fallback can be switched on today with no
# DNS at all:
#
#   LEAD_FROM_EMAIL="Jasmine Garcia Website <onboarding@resend.dev>"
#   LEAD_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL=<the address the Resend account was created with>
#
# Treat that as a stopgap: it is a shared sandbox domain, so deliverability is
# worse and it can reach exactly one recipient.
#
# The sandbox is behind us. The destination is jasmine@mattstoneteam.com (her
# choice, 2026-08-28 — it lands in the inbox she already works out of), which is
# not the address the Resend account was created with, so the sandbox would have
# refused it. Verifying the domain is what made an arbitrary recipient possible,
# and it is done.
#
# ✅ DOMAIN VERIFIED 2026-08-28. Confirmed in DNS: DKIM at
# resend._domainkey.jasminegarcia.com, and SPF plus MX on send.jasminegarcia.com
# (`v=spf1 include:_spf.forge.rmta.net ~all`). DMARC exists at the apex as
# `v=DMARC1; p=none` — monitoring, not enforcement, which is the right setting
# to start on.
#
# CORRECTION to an earlier warning in this file. It said Resend's SPF would have
# to be merged into Google Workspace's, because a domain may carry only one SPF
# record. The rule is real; the warning did not apply. Resend puts SPF on a
# `send.` subdomain rather than the apex, so it never touches the record
# Workspace would use and there was nothing to merge.
#
# What that does leave: the apex still has NO SPF record at all, so mail
# Workspace sends as @jasminegarcia.com is unauthenticated. That is a
# pre-existing gap unrelated to this site, and worth closing separately with
# `v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all` on the apex.
#
# DNS is at Squarespace Domains, on Google nameservers.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# WHO IT COMES FROM, and WHO IT GOES TO. Easy to transpose, so:
#   LEAD_FROM_EMAIL         → the From: header. A sender, never a destination.
#   LEAD_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL → the To: header. This is where a lead lands.
#
# app/api/lead/route.ts also sets replyTo to the visitor's own address, so
# replying to a notification reaches the lead rather than the website.
#
# Keep them different addresses. Sending from and to the same mailbox works but
# reads as a loop to some spam filters, and it makes the two roles impossible to
# tell apart later.
# A From: address needs a verified domain, not a real mailbox — nothing is
# expected to arrive there, and replyTo already points at the visitor. Making
# website@ an alias of her working inbox costs nothing and catches anyone who
# replies to the wrong header.
#
# Historical note, because the failure mode is not obvious: setting an
# @jasminegarcia.com sender before the domain was verified would not have failed
# at setup. It fails on the first real lead, when Resend rejects the send.
LEAD_FROM_EMAIL="Jasmine Garcia Website <website@jasminegarcia.com>"

# Her working inbox, chosen so leads land where she already triages (Bill,
# 2026-08-28). Two consequences worth holding in mind:
#
# 1. It is a brokerage-controlled mailbox. The lead is already in the
#    brokerage's Follow Up Boss, so this adds little exposure that did not
#    exist — but it does mean both copies of a lead now live in systems the
#    brokerage administers, and this email was the copy that did not. If an
#    independent record is ever wanted, Resend accepts an array of recipients
#    and app/api/lead/route.ts passes `to` straight through.
# 2. Sending across domains rather than to jasminegarcia.com from
#    jasminegarcia.com removes one spam heuristic: mail claiming to come from
#    your own domain but arriving from a third party is a classic spoof shape,
#    and that is part of why the first test landed in spam. It does not remove
#    the need for SPF and DKIM — mattstoneteam.com's filters will still judge
#    an unauthenticated sender.
LEAD_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL="jasmine@mattstoneteam.com"

# GA4 — public by design (ships in client JS), but keep it in env rather than
# hardcoded so staging/other checkouts don't report into the production
# property. Only fires when NODE_ENV=production (components/google-analytics.tsx).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-DHYEFNYELD
